WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
Hi, everyone. Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners Episode 18 for April 10th,

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:10.180
2026. Today's AI news is pretty exciting, and I promise no jargon. Let's go.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:14.220
So imagine you had a super creative teammate who could look at your TikTok video,

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:19.480
read your essay, and study a photo of your science project all at once. Then that teammate could

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:24.540
instantly suggest ways to make everything better together. That is basically what just happened.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.400
MetaSuperintelligence Labs just unveiled Muse Spark, the first model in their new Muse family.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.600
It is described as a natively multimodal reasoning model, which simply means it was built from

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.800
the start to understand and work with pictures, videos, text, and other types of information at

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:45.500
the same time, instead of handling them separately. Think of it like having a friend who does not

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.540
just read your essay, but can also look at your drawings, watch your TikTok, and then help you

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:56.900
improve all of them together. The model also uses something called thought compression. That is a way

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:02.520
to shrink down its own thinking steps, so it stays efficient. And it uses parallel agents,

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:07.900
basically multiple AI helpers that can work on different parts of a problem at once,

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:14.480
then combine their answers. This is a big deal because most older AI systems were mainly good at text.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.880
A multimodal model that can reason across different formats opens the door to far more creative

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.820
and useful applications. Students could upload a photo of their science experiment and get help

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:31.920
explaining it. Artists could describe an idea in words and have the AI suggest improvements

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:37.720
while looking at reference images. Game developers or YouTubers could get smarter assistance when

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:43.660
mixing visuals and stories. For you specifically, this points to a future where the AI tools you use

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:48.520
every day on your phone or laptop will feel less like a smart search box and more like a creative

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:54.920
teammate. The Meta AI app climbed from number 57 to number 5 on the App Store right after the launch,

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:58.960
showing lots of people are already excited to try it. You can try something right now,

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:05.120
open the Meta AI app or go to Meta.ai in your browser. No special account needed for basic use.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:11.160
Type a prompt that mixes words and an image. For example, upload a photo of your bedroom and ask

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:16.620
it to redesign this room in a cyberpunk style and explain your choices step by step. See how it

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:22.080
reasons across both the picture and your words. It is a fun way to experience multimodal AI without any

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:28.280
cost. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the Deep Dive. Today, we are going to unpack exactly

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:34.360
how multimodal AI actually works. And I promise we will start from the very beginning so it all makes

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:40.160
sense. You know how when you are texting your phone's auto-complete guesses the next word based on what

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:46.140
you have already typed. That is a super simple version of how language models work. They predict what

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:52.060
comes next. Now imagine instead of only seeing text, the AI gets trained on millions of pictures

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:58.800
paired with their descriptions, videos with captions, and spoken words turned into text. It learns patterns

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.780
that connect all these different languages. So when you show it a photo of a dog, it does not just

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:10.340
see pixels. It connects those pixels to all the words it has seen about dogs, sounds dogs make,

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:18.020
and even stories about dogs. The next clever step is that the model builds one shared understanding space

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:24.640
inside its mind. Whether the input is a sentence, a drawing, or a short video clip, it turns everything

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:31.020
into the same kind of internal signals. That lets it compare and combine ideas across formats. It is

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:36.480
like having one universal translator in your brain that can turn French music notes and emojis into

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.860
the same language so they can talk to each other. When Metacalls muse Spark natively multimodal,

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.380
they mean this mixing ability was designed in from the very beginning rather than added later.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:51.640
The model can create a visual chain of thought and basically showing its working by describing or

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:57.640
generating images as it thinks, which makes its reasoning easier to follow. So next time you hear

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:03.240
multimodal AI, you can tell your friends it is basically super advanced auto-complete that learn

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:07.120
to understand pictures, words, and sounds together instead of just guessing the next word.

